# Seller CMA letter copy

A seller letter must not print any of these. The generator refuses them in `lib/cma/seller-letter-copy.ts`. Do not put them back.

- Internal search labels, including `own-street-24mo` and any similar slug. Say it in plain English or omit the sentence.
- Unfinished sentences, including "before saying what."
- "the N of the M" (for example "the four of the five").
- Review tokens such as `pass`, and a line that says the sales support more than a failed ask while the printed recommendation is lower.
- A list date labeled as the off-market date.
- Two counts that do not each say what they count.
- A new-home sentence that does not match the table.
- A local price per square foot that held flat next to a large date cut. One story.
- A negligible-weight sale described as the sale that set the number. It stays in the table when the price counted it, with a line that its weight is under one percent.
- A zero room adjustment with no plain-language explanation.

No buyer or seller names.

- The count you use is the count you show. Four sales means the letter says four and the table shows four. Five means five, and the same for seven, eight, or three expired listings. A headline, sentence, or adjusted-price line never counts a row the table left off.
- Say a sale was added from the subject's own street only when that sale is on the subject's street. Name a sale in another subdivision, and name the subdivision. Do not call it the subject's street.
- Each home on the screen cards shows one listing photo when the MLS has one. Do not strip those photos.

Sold information includes seller concessions (or seller-paid closing costs) when the MLS already stored an amount. Comparison matrices factor that amount into the sale they adjust. A sale price that ignores a recorded concession is wrong. When the sale reported none, print none or zero; do not hide the line. Do not invent a concession amount. Adjusted matrix figures, the prices that set the recommendation, and any sold summary must use the same concession-adjusted sale.

A resale built within 5 years of the as-of date still prices with brand-new homes. That is the existing custom/new year window, not a new cutoff. Do not drop those never-owned sales from the price set. After 5 years, a resale with the new-construction flag false is not the same product as never-owned new construction. When the subject is a resale inside the window and the sales that set the price include never-owned new homes, the letter says this home is basically a new home, but it is competing with brand-new homes that have had no previous owner, at that same price point, so it will likely sell for less. No dollar discount is invented. The names in that sentence are homes in the price set.

Each sold comp field prints once on the phone card, in the same order as the column. Do not print Sold, Sold for, or seller concessions a second time in the adjustment block. A card must not paint another card's weight column. The room-adjustment sentence wraps in full. It is not clipped.
